    "title_english": "弟 — younger brother and harmony",
    "summary_english": "An associative character resembling a pestle wrapped in leather, commonly used for ‘younger brother’ in early texts.",
    "etymology": {
      "available": true,
      "headword": "弟",
      "pronunciation": "dì定纽、脂部；定纽、荠韵、徒礼切。定纽、脂部；定纽、霁韵、特计切。",
      "bibliography": [
        "《甲文编》256页",
        "《金文编》386页",
        "《战文编》350页",
        "《说文》113页",
        "《马王堆》221页"
      ],
      "explanation_original": "会意字。从\n（必）\n﹑从己，像柲缠韦形，《说文》以为“韋束之次弟也”。战国文字字形相沿袭，亦有一些变化，主要是柲形变直竖为向右\n（偶向左）\n的斜画，随之下端的一横也变成一撇；亦有不作一撇而作圆点的。缠韦形亦有简化。秦小篆﹑汉隶的写法都受战国文字的影响。弟古多用为兄弟之弟。\n《包山楚简》151：“步死，無子，其弟番󷄲\n（後）\n之。”\n《侯马盟书》309有“兄弟”一词。\n《诗·邶风·柏舟》：“亦有兄弟，不可以据。”\n战国文字中还有“敱弟”一词，信阳楚墓竹简1.011：“敱弟君子”，敱读豈，“豈弟”见《诗·齐风·载驱》，毛传训“豈弟”为“乐易也”。\n（刘桓）",
      "images_omitted": true,
      "explanation_english": [
        "An associative character, from （必） and 己, resembling a 柲 wrapped in leather; 《说文》 explains it as 韦束之次弟也. Warring States forms follow the inherited structure, with several changes: chiefly, the vertical 柲 form becomes a right-slanting (occasionally left-slanting) stroke, and its lower horizontal becomes a left-falling stroke; in some forms it is a round dot instead. The wrapped-leather form is also sometimes simplified. Qin small-seal and Han clerical forms were both influenced by Warring States writing. In antiquity 弟 was commonly used for the 弟 of 兄弟, a younger brother. 《包山楚简》151 reads: 步死，無子，其弟番󷄲（後）之。 《侯马盟书》309 contains the word 兄弟. 《诗·邶风·柏舟》 reads: 亦有兄弟，不可以据。 Warring States writing also has 敱弟: the Xinyang Chu tomb bamboo slip 1.011 reads: 敱弟君子. 敱 is read 豈; 豈弟 occurs in 《诗·齐风·载驱》, where Mao’s commentary defines 豈弟 as 乐易也. (Liu Huan)"
      ]
    },
